## Commands

- `task setup`: install the environment and git hooks. `task --list` shows all tasks.
- `task check`: lint, types, import contracts and tests. This is the merge gate.
- `task test -- <pytest args>`, `task lint`, `task example-data`, `task run`
  {%- if config == 'hydra' %} (Hydra overrides after `--`){% endif %}.
{%- if tracking == 'mlflow' %}
- `task mlflow`: MLflow UI for runs tracked in `data/tracking/`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if 'api' in interfaces %}
- `task api`: serve the REST API.
{%- endif %}
{%- if 'dashboard' in interfaces %}
- `task dashboard`: run the dashboard.
{%- endif %}
- `task docs:serve`: live documentation.
{%- if infrastructure == 'azure' %}
- `task infra:test`: test the Azure infrastructure in `infra/` with Pulumi mocks. `infra:preview`,
  `infra:up` and `infra:release` change real resources: run them only when asked.
{%- endif %}
- Dependencies: `uv add <pkg>`, or `uv add --group <dev|test|lint|docs> <pkg>` for tooling.
  Never use pip. Never edit `uv.lock` by hand.
- Turn repeated commands into named tasks in `Taskfile.yml`.

## Layout
{% if architecture == 'hexagonal' %}
`src/{{ package_name }}/` follows a hexagonal architecture. Dependencies point inward:
`entrypoints` → `bootstrap` → `adapters` → `application` → `ports` → `domain`.

- `domain/`: pure types and logic, no I/O
- `application/`: use cases, depending on ports and domain only
- `ports/`: protocols for outbound dependencies
- `adapters/`: implementations of ports (files, tracking, models)
- `bootstrap/`: settings and wiring, the only place that picks concrete adapters
- `entrypoints/`: command line{% if 'api' in interfaces %}, API{% endif %}{% if 'dashboard' in interfaces %}, dashboard{% endif %}

Each layer has an AGENTS.md. Read it before adding code there.
{%- elif architecture == 'pipeline' %}
`src/{{ package_name }}/pipelines/` holds the `features`, `training` and `inference` pipelines:
graphs of nodes (pure functions with named inputs and outputs), in the style of Kedro. `catalog.py` maps dataset names to
files (configured in `conf/`), `settings.py` holds typed settings, and `cli.py`{% if 'api' in interfaces %}, `api.py`{% endif %}{% if 'dashboard' in interfaces %}, `dashboard.py`{% endif %} are the entry
points. Read `pipelines/AGENTS.md` before adding a step or a pipeline.
{%- else %}
`src/{{ package_name }}/` holds flat modules: `data`, `model`, `evaluate`, `pipeline`,
`tracking`, `config` and the entry points (`cli`{% if 'api' in interfaces %}, `api`{% endif %}{% if 'dashboard' in interfaces %}, `dashboard`{% endif %}).
Entry points may import everything; nothing imports them.
{%- endif %}

- Import rules are enforced by import-linter (`[tool.importlinter]` in `pyproject.toml`).
- Tests: `tests/unit/` for logic, `tests/integration/` for component boundaries.
- Data: `data/raw/` is immutable input. Outputs go to `interim/`, `processed/`, `models/`
  and `reports/`. All of `data/` is git-ignored.

## Rules

- Test every meaningful behavior change in the same change, with the smallest test that gives
  confidence. Test behavior, not implementation. Say why when you add no test.
- Keep frameworks at the edges:
  {%- if config == 'hydra' %} Hydra only in entry points,{% endif %}
  {%- if tracking == 'mlflow' %} MLflow only in tracking code,{% endif %} web frameworks only in their
  entry point.
- Notebooks are for exploration. Move reusable logic into the package, with tests.
- Report only results from tracked runs or saved artifacts. Never state a number you did not
  produce.
- Google-style docstrings for public functions and classes.

## {{ use_case_titles[use_case] }} rules
{% if use_case == 'forecasting' %}
- Split by time only. Evaluate with rolling-origin backtests over several origins.
- Every feature must exist at the forecast origin. Mark covariates as past-only or known in
  advance.
- Beat a seasonal naive baseline first. Report errors per horizon.
- Report point and probabilistic metrics (for example MASE and quantile loss) with interval
  coverage.
{%- elif use_case == 'anomaly_detection' %}
- Keep a labeled hold-out that threshold tuning never touches.
- Evaluate events (detection delay, missed and false events), not only points.
- Calibrate thresholds on validation data and document the alert budget.
- Split by time or by asset so no series appears on both sides.
{%- elif use_case == 'optimization' %}
- Keep prediction and decision apart: models estimate, an optimizer decides.
- Check every recommendation against constraints and guardrails before reporting it.
- Record the objective in an ADR and state the trade-offs it ignores.
- Validate decisions on held-out periods or by simulation, not only by model fit.
{%- elif use_case == 'computer_vision' %}
- Split by subject, device or session, never by image.
- Version datasets and labels, and record the version with every training run.
- Check label quality on a sample before trusting metrics.
- Keep training and inference preprocessing identical. Test exported models for parity.
{%- elif use_case == 'genai' %}
- Keep an evaluation set with expected outcomes. Grade every prompt or model change against it.
- Version prompts like code. Record prompt version, model and parameters with each run.
- Prefer validated structured outputs over parsing free text.
- Track cost and latency per request. Send no personal data without an approved basis.
{%- elif use_case == 'tabular' %}
- Prevent target leakage: every feature must be known at prediction time.
- Split by group when rows are related, by time when the target drifts.
- Compare against a simple baseline and report calibration, not only accuracy.
- Report uncertainty for small samples.
{%- else %}
- Record each question, the data used and the answer.
- Conclusions cite the exact query or code that produced them.
- Report uncertainty and sample sizes with every estimate.
- Prefer simple, explainable methods unless a complex one is clearly justified.
{%- endif %}

## Planning and decisions

- Save design specs as `docs/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md` and plans as
  `docs/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md`. This replaces the `docs/superpowers/` default.
- When an approved design settles a durable decision (structure, objective, data or evaluation
  rules, what results may claim), record an ADR in `docs/adr/` before implementing. Link it
  from the design.
- Accepted ADRs outrank code and tests, which outrank plans.
- Execute approved plans with the `deliver` skill. Finish with `cross-review`.

## Definition of done

- `task check` passes.
- Changed behavior is tested. Docs and docstrings match the behavior.
- Conventional Commits (`type(scope): subject`), sized by responsibility. No agent attribution
  in commits or pull requests.

## Writing style

Applies to everything you write here: replies, docs, docstrings, comments, commit messages.

- Answer first. Details as bullets, one idea each. Numbered steps for procedures.
- No preamble, no restating the question, no process narration, no recap sections.
- No em dashes. Plain words over jargon; explain an unavoidable technical term briefly.
- One idea per sentence. Cut filler and redundant qualifiers.
- If unsure, say so and say how to find out.
- Precise, neutral, matter-of-fact tone.
